\DOC CONTAINED_ASM_TAC

\TYPE {CONTAINED_ASM_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves a {contained_modulo} containment goal outright from the numeric facts in
the assumption list.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is a {contained_modulo}
statement relating a (base, length) pair to an enclosing (base, length) pair,
it closes the goal:
{
   A ?- t
   ========  CONTAINED_ASM_TAC
   (no subgoals)
}
\noindent Internally it reduces the containment to an arithmetic implication
whose hypotheses are the arithmetic-shaped assumptions drawn from {A}, and
succeeds only when that implication is provable by linear arithmetic.

\FAILURE
Fails when the arithmetic facts extracted from the assumptions do not linearly
entail the required containment inequalities — for instance when the enclosing
region named in the conclusion is a different region from the one the
assumptions bound the address against, or when the bound depends on a
nonlinear/{MIN}-shaped quantity not derivable from the extracted hypotheses.
The failure surfaces as the arithmetic decision procedure reporting no
contradiction for the generated implication, so it arises from the tactic's own
arithmetic step rather than from any supplied argument.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show only this all-or-nothing behaviour, on conclusions of
this single containment form, with large assumption lists mixing arithmetic and
machine-state facts.

\ENDDOC
